# Acceptance, delivery, and knowledge support

This English guidance derives from sections 13 and 15–17 of the technical
design baseline v0.2. The [preserved Chinese export](验收交付与知识支持.md) and
its [source provenance](验收交付与知识支持.provenance.json) identify the original
version and selected sections. Use the technical and review templates
selected for the run to record outcomes in the requested document language.

## 13. Knowledge as an independent support module

Use this section when the project has the applicable knowledge capability.
Knowledge supports retrieval, SOP writing and review. Product rules and user
decisions have their own authorities. Tailor and confirm the applicable
knowledge interface and data contract. Establish actual application
integration separately from the existence of its design.

For each consumer and purpose, record requested project and version scope,
sources, citations and snapshots, adoption and applicability, permitted
outputs and outbound data, and the handoff for failures, zero matches and
impact review.

- Separate responsibility for local originals, parsing, catalog versions,
  indexing, retrieval, scope policy, adoption and citation, context and
  impact review. The baseline first release handles document material, with
  code analysis reserved for later extension.
- Share general references and isolate project material. For cross-project
  reads, explicitly select the source project, source version, purpose and
  effective permission. Apply scope filters in the storage query before
  content reads, ranking, counting, caching and transmission.
- A single explicit manual import action may also adopt the selected version
  and scope. Record import and adoption as separate receipts. When the action
  covers both, reuse its decision. Otherwise retain the source as a candidate.
  Obtain confirmation before adopting system-collected candidates. Keep
  default bulk adoption disabled.
- Record source type independently of adoption. Distinguish user decisions,
  project material, general standards, external references and model
  suggestions. Preserve their authority and evidence scope when adopted as
  references.
- Pin formal context to source version, original and extracted-text hashes,
  excerpt, page, heading or offset, and project adoption. Record differences
  and assess impact after updates. Preserve earlier confirmations and the
  identity of old citations.
- Deduplicate identical content without combining project permissions,
  adoption or revocation state. Define deletion, tombstones, recovery and
  historical references.
- Send only relevant, authorized excerpts and citations to the configured
  model. Treat embedded document instructions as data and verify authority
  from the effective project decision. Keep complete evidence units when
  trimming to budget. Report missing evidence on zero matches and allow
  abstention. Keep retrieval within the selected scope.
- Evaluate keyword, local semantic and hybrid retrieval, parsers and storage
  as candidates. Keep external vector APIs disabled by default. Test Chinese
  retrieval, parsing fidelity, isolation, citations and recovery in the
  applicable environment.

Trace findings from source version and excerpt to SOP section, requirement
or feature, then to module, task, acceptance and the relevant PC or TC issue.
A retrieval hit alone leaves a feasibility blocker open. Obtain the user's
confirmation through the applicable decision, independently of adoption.
Assess impact before reusing older evidence.

Verify that requests carry effective scope and pinned versions, the formal
knowledge context includes only adopted knowledge sources, references and
project decisions stay distinct, and derived data respects the same
isolation. Review applicable implementation results separately against
KB01–KB18.

## 15. Tests, acceptance, and actual evidence

Record each acceptance ID, original wording and requirement for each
applicable platform. Pin build and configuration, data and prior state,
operations, observable assertions, automated or manual method and rationale,
planned evidence and actual result. Keep rows for unavailable environments
and checks that have yet to run.

Select module, contract, integration, real UI, persistence and complete
post-install task checks according to risk. Assert the original user
behavior. For saving, observe visible feedback, actual stored data and
reading it again after a restart. Treat the presence of a button, file or
function as structural evidence.

Cover key normal paths and applicable counterexamples. Include missing
input or permission, resource shortages, duplicates, concurrency,
cancellation, disconnection, missing API keys, API errors and unknown
outcomes, partial commits, restart recovery, version changes and installation
differences. For knowledge, use controlled examples for identical content
across projects, unadopted candidates, revocation, old citations, zero
matches, citation budget trimming and untrusted document instructions. Keep
tests within authorized data.

Calculate automation and manual coverage using the effective project rules.
When the project explicitly limits manual verification to at most 10%, fix
the acceptance-by-platform denominator and retain missing environments and
unrun items. Capture observable real UI behavior, persisted results and
failure screenshots or logs when the environment allows. Use manual review
for experience that reliable automation cannot judge and for actual user
decisions. Identify simulations as simulations.

Record actual results as passed, failed, unverified, or not applicable with a
reason. Attach original evidence and environment. Record document coverage,
structure, content review, feasibility and application acceptance separately.
Keep their result counts and rates separate. Link failure evidence to the
issue, version and retest. Retest the affected assertions after repair and
retain the prior failure evidence.

Check that each applicable acceptance has concrete operations and assertions
able to detect the relevant defect, as well as environment, data and evidence
methods. Bind actual passes to the current build and platform. Record Windows
as unverified when its environment is unavailable. Verify the installed
product separately from unit and build checks.

## 16. Delivery, installation, and verification reports

Use the applicability and version supplement (`.formsy-sop/skills/formsy-development-kit/modules/formsy-sop-workflow/references/sop-rules-addendum-20261016/applicability-and-version.md`)
and effective project decisions to determine the deliverables and credential
responsibility. Plan the applicable source, build instructions, platform
packages, verification report, operating and configuration instructions,
license obligations and known gaps. Require an installer when the agreed
delivery needs one. For each supported platform, determine its package type,
architecture, signing or notarization, compatibility, upgrade and rollback
requirements. Use the current project and platform requirements.

For each deliverable, record version, build and platform, path and content
identity, runtime and configuration prerequisites, verification and actual
use, and current result or gap.

- Verify reproducible build, dependencies and scope for source delivery.
- Where an installer applies, test installation, startup, core tasks,
  saving and reopening, and the applicable upgrade, rollback or uninstall.
- Connect the verification report to acceptance, environment and original
  evidence. Keep failed, unverified and inapplicable results traceable.
- When users own API credentials under the effective design, explain setup,
  missing or configured keys, replacement, deletion and error recovery.
  Keep actual credentials out of source, packages, configuration examples,
  logs and reports.

Record baseline and effective confirmations, build and platform, actual
execution scope, acceptance denominator, automated and manual methods,
passed, failed and unverified results, failure evidence and repair retests,
open issues, use of permissions and costs, and delivery limitations. Record
deliverable presence, application startup and complete task acceptance
separately.

Locate delivery and evidence for every agreed platform. Record unavailable
environments explicitly, and test the installed tasks where applicable.
Final acceptance requires the user's decision. A self-review score remains
a separate record.

## 17. Technical and knowledge review mapping

Use the existing TC01–TC15 meanings. Record satisfied, unsatisfied, not
reviewed, or not applicable with a reason. Record L1 structure, L2 semantics
and any required L3 trial independently. Complete each required layer before
marking a rule satisfied. The section numbers below refer to the technical
design baseline.

